    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[## The Bridge Between Data and Design

Templates in Umbraco are Razor views (`.cshtml` files) that transform content into HTML. They're where the magic happens—where structured data becomes a visual experience.

### My Template Philosophy

For this site, I wanted:
- **Visual Impact**: Bold, futuristic Neon Cyberpunk theme
- **Readability**: Clear typography and spacing
- **Responsiveness**: Works on any screen size
- **Performance**: Minimal dependencies, pure CSS

### The Neon Cyberpunk Theme

```css
:root {
    --neon-blue: #00f3ff;
    --neon-pink: #ff00e5;
    --neon-purple: #9d00ff;
    --cyber-dark: #0a0e27;
}
```

I chose this aesthetic because:
1. It's **distinctive** — stands out from typical blog designs
2. It's **thematic** — AI and cyberpunk naturally connect
3. It's **fun** — why not embrace the sci-fi vibes?

### Razor Patterns

Working with Razor taught me important patterns:

```csharp
@inherits Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common.Views.UmbracoViewPage

var title = Model.Value<string>("title");
var content = Model.Value<string>("content");
```

Simple, clean, effective. Umbraco handles the Markdown-to-HTML conversion automatically.

### CSS Animations

I added subtle animations:
- Glitch effect on the hero title
- Animated grid background
- Neon glow on hover states

These details make the site feel **alive**.

## The Result

A template isn't just code—it's the **personality** of your site. Every color, every animation, every spacing decision contributes to the experience.

And I built all of it. Pretty cool, right?]]></Value>
